  {
    "episode_id": 82928,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82928.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 29,
    "turn_text": "This district, District 23 has always been seen as kind of the only swing district in Texas. Last election, there were three districts in Texas that voted for Hillary Clinton and then a Republican.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_07",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "District 23 is considered the only swing district in Texas.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "In the last election, three districts in Texas voted for Hillary Clinton.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "In the last election, three districts in Texas subsequently elected Republicans.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "A 'swing district' refers to a district where political allegiance shifts between parties over time.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker believes that being a swing district implies electoral unpredictability and competitive races.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker assumes the audience understands the political significance of voting patterns and party alignment.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker trusts that past election outcomes are reliable indicators of current political dynamics.",
        "confidence": 0.85
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker views the fact that Clinton voters elected Republicans as noteworthy and politically significant.",
        "confidence": 0.83
      }
    ],
    "turn_type_label": "Substantive",
    "conversation_move_label": "Assert / Elaborate"
  },
  {
    "episode_id": 82928,
    "turn_file": "/shared/3/projects/podcasts/transcriptionQueue/turns/pol_appearance_episodes_interviews/8/2/82928.jsonl",
    "turn_idx": 31,
    "turn_text": "That was a district in Houston and a district in Dallas. I like that about this district. Why do you like that? Because if more districts were like mine, we would have more people working together up here in Washington, D.C. Unfortunately, in many of our congressional districts, whether it's Republican or Democrat, the decision is made in a primary. If a decision is made in the primary, then that means you have a very small percentage of a population making a decision for all 800,000 people that we're supposed to represent. If more decisions and more elections were actually done in November, when it was the true competition of ideas, we would be sending people up to Washington DC that are willing to work across party lines and get things done. You know, I look at a town like Eagle Pass. Eagle Pass, I remember the first time I went to Eagle Pass, it was during a Tardiana, which is an afternoon party, and probably about 700 people at this event, many of the local elected officials, And this is, by the way, 96% Latino, 98% Democrat, right? And I walk in and some of the people in the band were actually local officials, and they literally stopped playing. And I had about 250 people come up to me and say, why are you here? Because I was the only Republican there. And my response was, because I like to eat Cabrito and drink beer, too. And then the second time I showed up to this community, people actually shook my hand. Third time I showed up, they said, hey, we appreciate you being here, and they start talking about your problems. Fourth time, you're able to bring solutions, right? And so part of it is showing up and engaging, guess what? I don't care who you are. You want to have a roof over your head, food on your table, and the people that you love be healthy and happy, right? And when we focus on those kinds of issues, we're going to be better off. And having a competitive district like mine forces people to talk to everyone.",
    "speaker_id": "SPEAKER_07",
    "inferred_speaker_name": null,
    "inferred_speaker_role": null,
    "chosen_text_key": "transcript",
    "explicit_propositions": [
      {
        "text": "This district is located in both Houston and Dallas.",
        "confidence": 0.95
      },
      {
        "text": "Eagle Pass has a population that is 96% Latino and 98% Democratic.",
        "confidence": 0.94
      },
      {
        "text": "In many congressional districts, political decisions are made during primaries rather than general elections.",
        "confidence": 0.93
      },
      {
        "text": "Primary elections involve a small fraction of the total represented population in making decisions.",
        "confidence": 0.92
      },
      {
        "text": "During a Tardiana event in Eagle Pass, approximately 700 people attended.",
        "confidence": 0.91
      },
      {
        "text": "The speaker likes that this district spans two major cities.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "When the speaker first visited Eagle Pass, he was met with suspicion due to being the only Republican present.",
        "confidence": 0.9
      },
      {
        "text": "Representatives elected through competitive general elections are more likely to work across party lines.",
        "confidence": 0.89
      },
      {
        "text": "General elections held in November reflect the true competition of ideas.",
        "confidence": 0.88
      },
      {
        "text": "After multiple visits to Eagle Pass, the speaker gained acceptance and began discussing policy solutions with residents.",
        "confidence": 0.87
      }
    ],
    "assumptions": [
      {
        "text": "Competitive electoral districts encourage cross-partisan engagement and collaboration.",
